  },
  {
    "episode_id": 92776,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/2/92776.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 32,
    "turn_text": "I know everybody in the press wants an answer to that. If we gave you an answer five minutes after the election, is everybody was asking for it or five days after the election? I mean, it would be kind of meaningless because this is something that requires real study and real analysis. You don't have a gut. A gut. Take here. Well, I'll tell you this. I know that our turnout was down. Trump's was up a little bit, but ours was down significantly. Down 10 or 11 million votes, okay? Those are people who, if they went to the polls, would be voting for us. But you guys had a great turnout machine. Well, to a certain extent we did in conventional terms, but the thing about politics is it's a moving object, right? So, yeah, I went to 27 states. I was involved in dozens of Canvas operations and rallies and so on. That's what felt to me psychologically like a great turnout machine, but maybe that's not what works today. Maybe social media and internet is far more important, or maybe we've been outdistanced in that respect. So we got to look at that. I don't know the answer to it. I do know that Democrats win when we've got economic progressive populism on our side and we lose when we are the cultural elite admonishing the public about people's language mistakes and stuff like that. Like all that is obviously true.",
