    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/2/92776.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 34,
    "turn_text": "Yes, the latter. But there we go. I mean, that's what it was. But we got to analyze how we got maneuvered into that position, right? I mean, I'm just saying we need a cold, hard political science look as best as we can, knowing that politics is not exclusively a science. It's also an art. but we need a cold hard look at what happened and where we need to make up ground pragmatically. I know there's a million people out there with an ideological act to grind who want to say it was this it was that look hey if you knew what the problem was all along you should have told us before you should have written your op-ed four months before the election, not four days after the election, right? So victory has a thousand parents and defeat is always an orphan. But what it means to be a serious political actor is to You know, cheer people up as much as you can. Get up off of the ground, dust yourself off and say, let's move forward and let's figure out, you know, all of the different views we can incorporate to move forward and win the next election. What else can we do? Winning is the name of the game. And it's never been more serious than it is right now. We know that everything is on the table. Everything's at stake.",
